 "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464628} {:answer/id #uuid "8d462a0f-93c1-5775-84c1-96d61ea828a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddd57b68-0ced-5792-aded-4f16279eb1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464628} :answer-field/id #uuid "d83acde9-9dda-58e0-a072-e702efca6af3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "951baf6c-974c-552a-a084-e87ba6cba5d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-159485" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4ad8b759d3f2781b1dc6adf64e58a230218655249f21fdd8f6a852568554ead.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/56/56a640baf27e1acb5c3428e1bef25a87b3ff965dff71762b94fb1eff94aaf24d.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $B$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f560f963-41fb-5961-a4ca-e7f4fae76b45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464630} {:answer/id #uuid "49e308bf-0f02-5eb9-b2bc-bf86afa295d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f560f963-41fb-5961-a4ca-e7f4fae76b45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464630} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d8e3c63-c305-5f9d-9fae-7be7bf8fa748" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465249} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fdb6dfd-403e-5409-ae24-c2f6b082e858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464632} {:answer/id #uuid "d0918475-615c-57e6-ae51-7a5a2dfca03a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0918475-615c-57e6-ae51-7a5a2dfca03a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464633} :answer-field/id #uuid "605fb6d1-632d-5c3c-9000-57b80aa8a7c6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465250} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43984d05-1815-5e12-9450-b7de96ee509c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464634} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e933cd-0038-5808-8fda-d3b46aec52cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1e933cd-0038-5808-8fda-d3b46aec52cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464635} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed08edab-6965-5dc6-8509-d235810e548d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e85ebcb1-0815-545b-a7ea-7afc898a561a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159502" :question/problem "![](images/79/79fc33355e32fc3b57a129e83fdf35ecdd1e5b0f3275c433a49d85642cf40f3f.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nII. $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nIII. $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/a7/a7f99060032c4f79df8b2ee68e220f31bb14f29f99e9190c9b385d073f3274d9.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is false. Every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ The point $A$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $B$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d436c48-b5c4-585f-a38c-5dc69c2efe0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} 