21-530a-b0ec-0053da9223ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-160647" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(5,-1),(2,0),(-2,-1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n- **I.** $(-1,-5)$\n- **II.** $(0,2)$\n- **III.** $(-1,-2)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-1,-5),(0,-2),(-1,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c65752e0-6e2e-5f72-b25d-1d72c342a96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465351} {:answer/id #uuid "eab58d28-c443-5176-a039-e1fa9838636c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answe