#edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "97308e70-f67e-5dc9-9884-0142720ba3a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-160648" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(1,-1),(-2,3),(-1,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n- **I.** $(1,1)$\n- **II.** $(-3,-2)$\n- **III.** $(0,2)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of ${90}^{∘}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,1),(-3,-2),(0,-1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ced6f09a-8000-5f7c-ad47-988971bb0974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218604549